    "result_text": "All tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added an optional owner `postcode` (4 digits), validated per the city's region only when present, keeping the request contract backward-compatible. The logic is split across small, single-responsibility units matching existing conventions:\n\n**Domain**\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 added the `postcode` field with `@Column`, getter/setter (alongside the other owner attributes).\n- `model/RegionPostcodes.java` (new) \u2014 the fixed region\u2192range table (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099) with `isValidForCity(city, postcode)`. It reuses the existing `Locality` city\u2192region mapping rather than duplicating it, and returns `true` for cities with no known region (the permissive default). This mirrors the existing `Locality` class.\n\n**Validation**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 declared `postcode` on the `OwnerFields` schema with `pattern: \"^[0-9]{4}$\"`, so a malformed value is rejected with 400 via the standard Bean Validation pass (the same declarative-constraint idiom the schema already uses for names/telephone).\n- `rest/controller/PostcodeValidator.java` (new) \u2014 a `Validator` handling the cross-field region-range rule (which can't be declarative), rejecting an out-of-range postcode through the same `BindingResult` \u2192 400 path as `OwnerFieldsValidator`. It skips absent/malformed values, leaving those to the `@Pattern` constraint.\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1` \u2014 registered the new validator on the owner binder alongside the existing one.\n\n**Persistence**\n- Added the `postcode` column to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres).\n\nMapStruct maps `postcode` automatically on both `toOwner` and `toOwnerDto` since the field names match, so it's stored and returned with no mapper changes.\n\nTest results: the three new acceptance tests (valid-postcode stored, absent-postcode accepted, malformed rejected with 400) pass, and the full suite is green.",
